    "25b0152eb0224cc8800a2d0284f2b62c6bab8d4933b1122da4e834e7bd922fdf": "```txt\nLegacy status: Superseded pointer\nSuperseded by:\n- docs/architect/workspace-topology.md\n- docs/platform/packaging-distribution/architecture/runtime-identity-and-activation.md\nCurrent reader entry: docs/platform/packaging-distribution/README.md\nUse this legacy file for: Redirect only\nDo not use this legacy file for: Current model detail\nLast reviewed: 2026-09-13\n```",
    "92f7ecb75495a4920083dbdaf2dd9d233995244c3e014092619b02dd0abf1bb6": "## Upgrade And Advisory Procedure\n\n- **2026-10-16 — fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; YAML is read through one vendored parser (RUL101 (fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; every YAML parse goes through one vendored js-yaml 5.4.3 behind src/util/yaml.mjs)).** Before this, `yaml` was the one production dependency. An installed release carried it (the release builder stages declared production dependencies), but a checkout, linked worktree or unpacked copy without `npm install` did not, so every module importing it failed to load there, and the doctor and setup paths grew their own YAML readers and \"not evaluated\" branches to keep loading. Decision: `package.json#dependencies` stays empty; the only YAML parser is `src/util/third-party/js-yaml.mjs`, the ESM bundle from the js-yaml 5.4.3 tarball, unmodified except a header and the dropped source-map comment line, and every parse goes through `src/util/yaml.mjs` (`parseYaml(text, { filename })`: zero documents give `null`, more than one throws, `maxAliases` 100, js-yaml's default core schema). An architecture test fails on any other import of `yaml`, `js-yaml` or the third-party file. Rejected: keeping `yaml` and making doctor tolerate its absence (what the workarounds did, and the cause of the divergence); a hand-rolled parser (a second authority for what YAML means); `vendor/` or `dist/` as the location (the repository's scout-block hook refuses those path segments, so agents could not read or review the file); 5.4.2 (a block-indentation mis-parse fixed in 5.4.3) and 5.5.0 (one day old at the time). Readers that used to skip silently now report a parse failure as a named problem with file, line and column; pinned persona text that the strict parser rejects renders through a private legacy projection so older runs resume with the same text (its removal is tracked in the backlog). Evidence: commits `8ef1baf1c` (door and vendored file), `838b044e5` (call sites), `b75c01a3f` (release and dev-host staging), `6013ca389` (dependency dropped), `f96eece77`, `b7eeb4bb6` and `26b3338a7` (readers use the parser), `f705b9c8c` (doctor checks `compiled.json`); a parity run of the old parser against the door over every tracked YAML and frontmatter document found 828 documents, 0 differences, 13 rejected by both; a cold `fgos` start costs 2 to 3 ms more (97 to 99 ms for `version`, 109 to 110 ms for `dispatch decide`); the full suite ran 7850 tests with 0 failures with release binaries present; `test/util/yaml.test.mjs` pins the vendored bytes by hash. Kept on their own paths: `src/report/frontmatter.mjs` pairs `parseFrontmatter` with `renderFrontmatter` over a deliberately flat schema, so replacing only the parser would make the pair asymmetric; `distill.mjs` carries a minimal YAML reader because portable skills ship without `src/`; `domains/*/compiled.json` stays a generated projection because parsing domain YAML at import would put every verb, hook and `doctor` at the mercy of one YAML typo (doctor now checks its freshness instead). Upgrade procedure: in an empty scratch directory run `npm pack js-yaml@<version> --ignore-scripts`; compare the tarball's sha512 with `npm view js-yaml@<version> dist.integrity`; extract the `.mjs` bundle from the tarball without executing anything in it (build the path so the hook-refused segment does not appear in the command); confirm it has no import or require; diff it against the current bundle and read every changed line; re-run the parity comparison against the previous parser over the tracked YAML; replace the third-party file with the new bundle under a fresh header (source, version, integrity, license, hash); update the hash constant in `test/util/yaml.test.mjs`. js-yaml advisories are watched through the backlog, since `npm audit` no longer covers the file.",
    "ae171397d00713803384cf611d8c65085c24a3e4c56589b49215d1c602b0bcb8": "Document type: Decision and maintenance procedure\nOwner: Packaging-distribution\nStatus: Conserved current parser maintenance policy; candidate authority follows the transitional switchboard.\nSource: `docs/specs/distribution.md`, 2026-10-16 decision, carried verbatim at `3e2d05af89313dbfbf0d26cd0de34f6b660d194b`.",
    "239a3f8b155d3cf4d3008ac3fbd63038aa180d922b8a14e7e884d5bc4bf7bbd3": "- **2026-10-16 — fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; YAML is read through one vendored parser (RUL101 (fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; every YAML parse goes through one vendored js-yaml 5.4.3 behind src/util/yaml.mjs)).** Before this, `yaml` was the one production dependency. An installed release carried it (the release builder stages declared production dependencies), but a checkout, linked worktree or unpacked copy without `npm install` did not, so every module importing it failed to load there, and the doctor and setup paths grew their own YAML readers and \"not evaluated\" branches to keep loading. Decision: `package.json#dependencies` stays empty; the only YAML parser is `src/util/third-party/js-yaml.mjs`, the ESM bundle from the js-yaml 5.4.3 tarball, unmodified except a header and the dropped source-map comment line, and every parse goes through `src/util/yaml.mjs` (`parseYaml(text, { filename })`: zero documents give `null`, more than one throws, `maxAliases` 100, js-yaml's default core schema). An architecture test fails on any other import of `yaml`, `js-yaml` or the third-party file. Rejected: keeping `yaml` and making doctor tolerate its absence (what the workarounds did, and the cause of the divergence); a hand-rolled parser (a second authority for what YAML means); `vendor/` or `dist/` as the location (the repository's scout-block hook refuses those path segments, so agents could not read or review the file); 5.4.2 (a block-indentation mis-parse fixed in 5.4.3) and 5.5.0 (one day old at the time). Readers that used to skip silently now report a parse failure as a named problem with file, line and column; pinned persona text that the strict parser rejects renders through a private legacy projection so older runs resume with the same text (its removal is tracked in the backlog). Evidence: commits `8ef1baf1c` (door and vendored file), `838b044e5` (call sites), `b75c01a3f` (release and dev-host staging), `6013ca389` (dependency dropped), `f96eece77`, `b7eeb4bb6` and `26b3338a7` (readers use the parser), `f705b9c8c` (doctor checks `compiled.json`); a parity run of the old parser against the door over every tracked YAML and frontmatter document found 828 documents, 0 differences, 13 rejected by both; a cold `fgos` start costs 2 to 3 ms more (97 to 99 ms for `version`, 109 to 110 ms for `dispatch decide`); the full suite ran 7850 tests with 0 failures with release binaries present; `test/util/yaml.test.mjs` pins the vendored bytes by hash. Kept on their own paths: `src/report/frontmatter.mjs` pairs `parseFrontmatter` with `renderFrontmatter` over a deliberately flat schema, so replacing only the parser would make the pair asymmetric; `distill.mjs` carries a minimal YAML reader because portable skills ship without `src/`; `domains/*/compiled.json` stays a generated projection because parsing domain YAML at import would put every verb, hook and `doctor` at the mercy of one YAML typo (doctor now checks its freshness instead). Upgrade procedure: in an empty scratch directory run `npm pack js-yaml@<version> --ignore-scripts`; compare the tarball's sha512 with `npm view js-yaml@<version> dist.integrity`; extract the `.mjs` bundle from the tarball without executing anything in it (build the path so the hook-refused segment does not appear in the command); confirm it has no import or require; diff it against the current bundle and read every changed line; re-run the parity comparison against the previous parser over the tracked YAML; replace the third-party file with the new bundle under a fresh header (source, version, integrity, license, hash); update the hash constant in `test/util/yaml.test.mjs`. js-yaml advisories are watched through the backlog, since `npm audit` no longer covers the file.",
    "cd9cf2285fed0ea03fb5e15a609e5a927374b38f533f00e814d6867e0a81f34c": "## Upgrade And Advisory Procedure\n\nThe complete former RUL101 narrative and upgrade procedure have one holder in\n[the distribution decision history](../spec.md#lịch-sử-quyết-định), explicitly\nmarked superseded. They are not current maintenance instructions.\n\nCurrent YAML reads follow RUL108 in [Dependency Upgrade Discipline](../spec.md#dependency-upgrade-discipline)\nand the [YAML Document contract](../../yaml-document/spec.md). The parser upgrade\nrecord is `packages/yaml-doc/upgrade-record.md` under RUL107; there is no JavaScript\nfallback, vendored js-yaml bundle or bundle-hash test to maintain.",
    "50af62f64bbaed6ea8dcb72f0152d1c1548d4b6f40ea2f14a5e6ac2476d4dc7b": "Target architecture has no separate `fgos setup` verb for workspace onboarding. `fgctl init` is the one-command workspace onboarding orchestrator; after activation it invokes active local `fgos init`, `fgos doctor --fix`, and `fgos doctor`. Legacy shell/global integration remains compatibility behavior under the explicit deprecated fallback window: 30 calendar days after preview release publication, unless a later release decision replaces that policy.",
    "198fdf63b31cf45c96e959d9d7fe5a3343074c09d29d55de498c38e7d44b4c9f": "## 2. Registry Types\n\n| Registry | Function | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Check | `registerCheck` | A named diagnostic reported by `fgos doctor`. |\n| Fix | `registerFix` | A named repair run by `fgos doctor --fix`; current legacy `fgos setup` also runs fixes while it remains implemented. |\n| Config default | `registerConfigDefault` | A default config shape merged into shared config without overwriting user values. |\n\n`event-store` registers the `eventStore` config shape `{ remote: \"origin\" }`. Config merge fills missing keys while preserving explicit remote choices; the `git-repository-config` check diagnoses obsolete exact legacy mappings on the configured remote and on historical `origin`, and the `git-publication-refspecs` fix repairs them.\n\nThe registries are independent. A module may register only a check, only a fix, only a config default, or any combination.",
    "4a0503739783d780322bb863de22c0b1c3b981cb608cc14f645041a3647197a2": "## 2. Registry Types\n\n| Registry | Function | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Check | `registerCheck` | A named diagnostic reported by `fgos doctor`. |\n| Fix | `registerFix` | A named repair run by `fgos doctor --fix`; current legacy `fgos setup` also runs fixes while it remains implemented. |\n| Config default | `registerConfigDefault` | A default config shape merged into shared config without overwriting user values. |\n\n`event-store` registers the `eventStore` config shape `{ remote: \"origin\" }`. Config merge fills missing keys while preserving explicit remote choices; `git-publication-refspecs` diagnoses and repairs obsolete exact legacy mappings on the configured remote and on historical `origin`.\n\nThe registries are independent. A module may register only a check, only a fix, only a config default, or any combination.",
    "e90bc6e26e222a5c4bc8cc58ad5256d3b266c5dfb7d397eeb3111d4742687686": "| Registry | Function | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Check | `registerCheck` | A named diagnostic reported by `fgos doctor`. |\n| Fix | `registerFix` | A named repair run by `fgos doctor --fix`; current legacy `fgos setup` also runs fixes while it remains implemented. |\n| Config default | `registerConfigDefault` | A default config shape merged into shared config without overwriting user values. |",
    "03024c345457b1dd391e1db4abb02b4ac98212f6a14ed4f25250b38a10e08861": "`event-store` registers the `eventStore` config shape `{ remote: \"origin\" }`. Config merge fills missing keys while preserving explicit remote choices; the `git-repository-config` check diagnoses obsolete exact legacy mappings on the configured remote and on historical `origin`, and the `git-publication-refspecs` fix repairs them.",
    "f9fb2d29ed2b8a633fae6352b66f16fad856d7f02221f82c4ce86f3ff9d3a050": "Import 2 current-byte rebind; full delta and neighbours: plans/260925-documentation-authority-unification/reports/phase-09/import2/binding-refresh.json; changed complete block in same ancestry; full before/after and both neighbouring blocks retained for review. Earlier source snapshots are provenance, not exact current-text assertions.",
    "ca4c40283c79b3196c6fc899257df5e482f1cdeb39b8ede6c863bf16d03524af": "## 3. Doctor Contract\n\n`fgos doctor`:\n\n- runs registered checks;\n- reports individual pass/fail status as a row `{id, description, passed, message, detail?}`; `detail` is an optional JSON value that holds the facts behind the row's one-sentence `message` (for example the versions and floor behind a compatibility failure) and appears only in `fgos doctor --json`;\n- does not write files by default;\n- should tell the user what command or fix path applies when a check fails.\n\nRun coverage is checked by `observe-run-coverage`: the host's `metrics coverage`\nmust report layout rule `v2` and valid observed/skipped accounting. Doctor\nindependently compares both eligible unique results and candidate-directory/\nbarrier counts against the Node production admission projection and layout scan.\nBoth read the main checkout store, including from a worktree. A candidate run\ndirectory changed in the last 60 seconds permits a bounded transient difference,\neven before a result exists; future mtimes are recent for clock skew. The host's\n`recentRuns` remains result-file based. Diagnostic paths are sample candidates,\nnot verified host omissions. An unknown coverage subcommand or a response\nwithout `layoutRule` identifies an old host positively and reports\npassed-but-degraded with an upgrade instruction; other host errors fail.\nThis check is read-only, introduces no config default, and never rewrites runs.\n\n`convention-conformance` checks the packaged naming and placement rules only\ninside an fgOS source checkout. Violations on paths already present at the\nrule cutoff pass with counts and up to three examples; a violation on a path\nintroduced after that cutoff fails the check, even while the rule posture is\n`warn`. A missing or old host pass-skips with the reason because doctor never\nreimplements the native rules. The check is read-only and has no registered\nfix or config default.\n\n\n`active-release-matches-checkout` compares a source checkout's Node payload\nworking-tree bytes with its own activated manifest, using the release builder's\nsource enumeration. It reports changed, missing and extra source files, excludes\nstaged production dependencies, and does not certify Rust host freshness.\nOutside source checkouts, without activation, with a same-checkout development\nmanifest, or in a linked worktree whose activation belongs to main, it reports\na reasoned skip. Unsafe paths and unreadable inputs fail this check rather than\nthrowing out the whole doctor run. It is read-only, with no registered fix:\n`npm run fgos:dev -- <verb>` runs working-tree code; build/stage/upgrade updates\nplain `fgos`. Diagnosis never changes the activation implicitly.\n\n\n| Check | Pass | Fail | Not applicable |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `documentation-cutover-lease` | Repository hooks are wired, the committed main-ref sidecar is absent, released or abandoned, and no stray main token exists. | Committed sidecar is corrupt/unreadable; a token exists in main while main is not the recorded holder; applicable hook wiring is missing; or a lease is held. Held state reports age and frozen scope, or a missing hook/kernel lock resource. | Outside Git or without a main branch; also without `.githooks/reference-transaction` when authority is neither held nor corrupt and no stray main token exists. |\n\nThis check reads committed main-ref authority before treating missing repository hooks as not applicable: held/corrupt authority still fails if the reference hook disappears. It diagnoses a stray main token by file presence and the recorded holder checkout, without validating a linked holder's token possession. Acquire refuses the same stray condition with `stray-main-token`; held-lease admission also refuses main callers with that residue. The check is read-only, with no registered fix, config default or automatic token cleanup. After checking committed state and the recorded holder, the owner removes residue by hand; a real holder's release restores any pre-existing main token bytes. The [lease contract](../../holder-lock/spec.md#documentation-cutover-write-lease) owns the release crash window and recovery.\n\n`fgos doctor --fix`:\n\n- runs every registered fix;\n- reports fix results;\n- then reports checks.",
    "214604117def14505e9ba4ab3815d287ffd0adee4a3304170626717db62d86de": "## 3. Doctor Contract\n\n`fgos doctor`:\n\n- runs registered checks;\n- reports individual pass/fail status;\n- does not write files by default;\n- should tell the user what command or fix path applies when a check fails.\n\nRun coverage is checked by `observe-run-coverage`: the host's `metrics coverage`\nmust report layout rule `v2` and valid observed/skipped accounting. Doctor\nindependently compares both eligible unique results and candidate-directory/\nbarrier counts against the Node production admission projection and layout scan.\nBoth read the main checkout store, including from a worktree. A candidate run\ndirectory changed in the last 60 seconds permits a bounded transient difference,\neven before a result exists; future mtimes are recent for clock skew. The host's\n`recentRuns` remains result-file based. Diagnostic paths are sample candidates,\nnot verified host omissions. An unknown coverage subcommand or a response\nwithout `layoutRule` identifies an old host positively and reports\npassed-but-degraded with an upgrade instruction; other host errors fail.\nThis check is read-only, introduces no config default, and never rewrites runs.\n\n`convention-conformance` checks the packaged naming and placement rules only\ninside an fgOS source checkout. Violations on paths already present at the\nrule cutoff pass with counts and up to three examples; a violation on a path\nintroduced after that cutoff fails the check, even while the rule posture is\n`warn`. A missing or old host pass-skips with the reason because doctor never\nreimplements the native rules. The check is read-only and has no registered\nfix or config default.\n\n\n`active-release-matches-checkout` compares a source checkout's Node payload\nworking-tree bytes with its own activated manifest, using the release builder's\nsource enumeration. It reports changed, missing and extra source files, excludes\nstaged production dependencies, and does not certify Rust host freshness.\nOutside source checkouts, without activation, with a same-checkout development\nmanifest, or in a linked worktree whose activation belongs to main, it reports\na reasoned skip. Unsafe paths and unreadable inputs fail this check rather than\nthrowing out the whole doctor run. It is read-only, with no registered fix:\n`npm run fgos:dev -- <verb>` runs working-tree code; build/stage/upgrade updates\nplain `fgos`. Diagnosis never changes the activation implicitly.\n\n\n| Check | Pass | Fail | Not applicable |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `documentation-cutover-lease` | Repository hooks are wired, the committed main-ref sidecar is absent, released or abandoned, and no stray main token exists. | Committed sidecar is corrupt/unreadable; a token exists in main while main is not the recorded holder; applicable hook wiring is missing; or a lease is held. Held state reports age and frozen scope, or a missing hook/kernel lock resource. | Outside Git or without a main branch; also without `.githooks/reference-transaction` when authority is neither held nor corrupt and no stray main token exists. |\n\nThis check reads committed main-ref authority before treating missing repository hooks as not applicable: held/corrupt authority still fails if the reference hook disappears. It diagnoses a stray main token by file presence and the recorded holder checkout, without validating a linked holder's token possession. Acquire refuses the same stray condition with `stray-main-token`; held-lease admission also refuses main callers with that residue. The check is read-only, with no registered fix, config default or automatic token cleanup. After checking committed state and the recorded holder, the owner removes residue by hand; a real holder's release restores any pre-existing main token bytes. The [lease contract](../../holder-lock/spec.md#documentation-cutover-write-lease) owns the release crash window and recovery.\n\n`fgos doctor --fix`:\n\n- runs every registered fix;\n- reports fix results;\n- then reports checks.",
    "1054adf2ffc0c9bd235e0f376f683364ebf733be8cec969ecccf85900777b599": "`fgos doctor`:\n\n- runs registered checks;\n- reports individual pass/fail status as a row `{id, description, passed, message, detail?}`; `detail` is an optional JSON value that holds the facts behind the row's one-sentence `message` (for example the versions and floor behind a compatibility failure) and appears only in `fgos doctor --json`;\n- does not write files by default;\n- should tell the user what command or fix path applies when a check fails.",
    "026a432df485ec60f2e3f10080b772c10d8ffe5298dcb4da289ff9b032af553a": "Run coverage is checked by `observe-run-coverage`: the host's `metrics coverage`\nmust report layout rule `v2` and valid observed/skipped accounting. Doctor\nindependently compares both eligible unique results and candidate-directory/\nbarrier counts against the Node production admission projection and layout scan.\nBoth read the main checkout store, including from a worktree. A candidate run\ndirectory changed in the last 60 seconds permits a bounded transient difference,\neven before a result exists; future mtimes are recent for clock skew. The host's\n`recentRuns` remains result-file based. Diagnostic paths are sample candidates,\nnot verified host omissions. An unknown coverage subcommand or a response\nwithout `layoutRule` identifies an old host positively and reports\npassed-but-degraded with an upgrade instruction; other host errors fail.\nThis check is read-only, introduces no config default, and never rewrites runs.",
    "5e78d2354bf6b6426c2ef8a99d43a0e3983990997bb7df7dd989d203798e7d3c": "`convention-conformance` checks the packaged naming and placement rules only\ninside an fgOS source checkout. Violations on paths already present at the\nrule cutoff pass with counts and up to three examples; a violation on a path\nintroduced after that cutoff fails the check, even while the rule posture is\n`warn`. A missing or old host pass-skips with the reason because doctor never\nreimplements the native rules. The check is read-only and has no registered\nfix or config default.",
