    "802724f22a0449402d5a00c82d4d841abeb507d4de0043083a6d0421edc451d8": "The Git checks share one ABI/floor row, `git-component-compatible`. `git-operation-journal` reports pending count, oldest unresolved operation and retained bytes, refuses unreadable or incomplete scans, and fails `git-fence-missing` (next step: `fgctl upgrade` for the active release) when a WAL exists without the controller activation fence. Native locks are detected without removal. `git-wal-dir` initializes the trusted WAL directories; `git-driver-directories` creates or tightens only the current user’s designated fgOS root and empty driver hooks directory, canonicalizing a symlinked state root, rejecting symlinked hooks/foreign owners and leaving parents untouched; existing root chmod additionally requires canonical basename `fgos` or the driver’s own floor-record marker; Git doctor refusals name the mode/owner cause and this registered fix. `git-publication-refspecs` reads the canonical Git RepositoryConfig mapping for the effective `eventStore.remote` (project over global, default `origin`), installs missing wildcard mirror mappings and replaces the obsolete exact legacy line using RepositoryConfig under repo-config 80, preserving user configuration and recording the effective remote URL. `event-store` registers `eventStore.remote` with default `origin`; obsolete exact origin mappings are also repaired when a different remote is configured. Reserved legacy-suffix mirrors are reported for manual cleanup. A missing addon makes the checks fail and fixes report a refusal without aborting unrelated setup fixes. Durability reports the process-crash/fsync contract without certifying stable media.",
    "f0daa55efc27d11e7d0a943a0c0453c151117e1319731444a19aa6900c2c8f88": "The Git checks share one ABI/floor row, `git-component-compatible`, which also prints the repository layout and measured durability as information. `git-repository-config` judges the repository's transport settings and the Event Store mirror fetch mappings together. `git-operation-journal` keeps the pending count, oldest unresolved operation and retained bytes under `detail`, refuses unreadable or incomplete scans, warns about retained Land candidates, and fails `git-fence-missing` (next step: `fgctl upgrade` for the active release) when a WAL exists without the controller activation fence. Native locks are detected without removal. `git-wal-dir` initializes the trusted WAL directories; `git-driver-directories` creates or tightens only the current user’s designated fgOS root and empty driver hooks directory, canonicalizing a symlinked state root, rejecting symlinked hooks/foreign owners and leaving parents untouched; existing root chmod additionally requires canonical basename `fgos` or the driver’s own floor-record marker; Git doctor refusals name the mode/owner cause and this registered fix. The `git-publication-refspecs` fix reads the canonical Git RepositoryConfig mapping for the effective `eventStore.remote` (project over global, default `origin`), installs missing wildcard mirror mappings and replaces the obsolete exact legacy line using RepositoryConfig under repo-config 80, preserving user configuration and recording the effective remote URL. `event-store` registers `eventStore.remote` with default `origin`; obsolete exact origin mappings are also repaired when a different remote is configured. Reserved legacy-suffix mirrors are reported for manual cleanup. A missing addon makes the checks fail and fixes report a refusal without aborting unrelated setup fixes. The durability line of `git-component-compatible` reports the process-crash/fsync contract without certifying stable media.",
    "97a1638d914aebc9cd1597dc14e2086b420c7491f5fcccaef71ba9ea18b8eb4e": "`git-transport-config` reports repository-local transport settings that Publication/Remote refuse, using Rust’s scope-based admission policy. For actions/checkout credentials, use `persist-credentials: false`, remove the repo-local `http.<url>.extraheader`, and configure a trusted global credential helper or `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`-level setting. The check is read-only, reports keys without credential values, and has no automatic credential migration.",
    "e97f05e5ec4bbe570cbff86a5103332090925a47df59d372d882eed71fec2178": "`git-repository-config` reports repository-local transport settings that Publication/Remote refuse, using Rust’s scope-based admission policy. For actions/checkout credentials, use `persist-credentials: false`, remove the repo-local `http.<url>.extraheader`, and configure a trusted global credential helper or `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`-level setting. The check is read-only, reports keys without credential values, and has no automatic credential migration.",
    "4ac7e67b347d3866327abc579d49350ed0af7279e016bedb2b7f2a83aa541694": "## Retained Distribution Source Detail\n\nAdded in candidate — complete source units from `docs/specs/distribution.md` at blob `13d2e172de35a570d148022d4c859cac05223acb`, after the boundary main sync. Dated decisions, implementation rules and recorded gaps are retained; current assertions remain subject to the independent truth sample.",
    "91a139f1a132bd3ad440efc8beac3a0f0a77e27326dc8f696a43acace808775d": "- **Blocked when:** never — this is an explicit command a contributor runs\n  themselves; it always runs when invoked.\n- **What changes:** running the setup command wires up this repository's\n  pre-commit hook for the person who just cloned it. It is never triggered\n  automatically by any package manager's install step (per str88-fgos-pnpm-lifecycle)\n  — a contributor runs it once, by hand, after cloning.\n- **Side effects:** none beyond the local git config change; nothing is\n  installed and no network access happens.\n- **Afterwards:** the contributor's local clone has the pre-commit hook\n  wired up, identically to what used to happen automatically. Someone who\n  never runs this command simply does not get the local hook — this is a\n  one-time manual step for contributors, not a requirement for installing\n  or running `fgos` itself.\n- In fgOS source checkouts, the hook refuses newly staged root files outside\n  its own allowlist, including linked worktrees and `fgw/*` branches. Existing\n  root modifications/deletions remain permitted. Allowlist changes must land\n  separately on main before root additions use them; the index cannot authorize\n  itself. Merge commits skip only this root guard, never existing data-loss\n  guards. `main-checkout-hook-wired` diagnoses wiring using the existing check.\n- In fgOS source checkouts, the hook sends newly staged, copied, or renamed\n  Markdown files directly under `plans/reports/` and `plans/journals/` to the\n  native Convention checker. Violations and unavailable, old, or failed hosts\n  emit warnings only; the hook never blocks the commit for Convention results.\n  Nested evidence and per-plan `reports/` directories are outside this scope.",
    "e18589434867dc92bb062b361a869145873f628b6908e0a4da9fd8db7c8417d2": "##### Contributor hooks setup\n\n- **Blocked when:** never — this is an explicit command a contributor runs\n  themselves; it always runs when invoked.\n- **What changes:** running the setup command wires up this repository's\n  pre-commit hook for the person who just cloned it. It is never triggered\n  automatically by any package manager's install step (per str88-fgos-pnpm-lifecycle)\n  — a contributor runs it once, by hand, after cloning.\n- **Side effects:** none beyond the local git config change; nothing is\n  installed and no network access happens.\n- **Afterwards:** the contributor's local clone has the pre-commit hook\n  wired up, identically to what used to happen automatically. Someone who\n  never runs this command simply does not get the local hook — this is a\n  one-time manual step for contributors, not a requirement for installing\n  or running `fgos` itself.\n- In fgOS source checkouts, the hook refuses newly staged root files outside\n  its own allowlist, including linked worktrees and `fgw/*` branches. Existing\n  root modifications/deletions remain permitted. Allowlist changes must land\n  separately on main before root additions use them; the index cannot authorize\n  itself. Merge commits skip only this root guard, never existing data-loss\n  guards. `main-checkout-hook-wired` diagnoses wiring using the existing check.\n- In fgOS source checkouts, the hook sends newly staged, copied, or renamed\n  Markdown files directly under `plans/reports/` and `plans/journals/` to the\n  native Convention checker. Violations and unavailable, old, or failed hosts\n  emit warnings only; the hook never blocks the commit for Convention results.\n  Nested evidence and per-plan `reports/` directories are outside this scope.",
    "f97dc0a27bfe9b69110326408c03051a91a414b6678bacb4f96ad696a311455f": "##### Setup\n\n- **Blocked when:** never — running `fgos setup` always attempts its work;\n  a shell profile that does not exist is simply skipped rather than\n  refused.\n- **What changes:** for every shell profile the caller actually has (bash's\n  and/or zsh's, whichever exist), the shell helper's source line is added\n  if not already present — never duplicated on a repeat run. The local\n  project config file is also brought up to date: any setting present in\n  the current default schema but missing from the caller's file is added,\n  without ever changing a setting the caller already customized; a config\n  file that already has every current default is left untouched. The same\n  fill-missing-only treatment is applied to the **global** config file\n  (`~/.fgos/config.json`, tsk-1ri) — `fgos setup` initializes it with the\n  same default schema the project-local file gets, or fills in any missing\n  key, every time it runs, regardless of which project it's run from;\n  a value the caller already customized at the global level is never\n  overwritten. The lock directories of row 5g are created when missing:\n  `<lockRoot>` and `<fgosDir>/locks`, both mode 0700, an existing one tightened to it (the `lock-dirs` fix);\n  the `terminal` section of row 5h is one of the defaults the\n  fill-missing-only merge adds; `<stateRoot>/terminal` is created with mode 0700 (the\n  `terminal-dir` fix).\n- **Side effects:** the caller's own shell profile file(s), local project\n  config file, global config file (`~/.fgos/config.json`), the two lock\n  directories and `<stateRoot>/terminal` may be created or modified; nothing outside the caller's own\n  environment is touched, and no network access happens.\n- **Afterwards:** the caller sees exactly what changed — which profile\n  file(s) gained the source line (or already had it), which project config\n  settings were newly added (or that none were needed), and which global\n  config settings were newly added (or that none were needed, or that the\n  global file was just created). Nothing is done silently; running `fgos\n  setup` again when everything is already current reports that plainly,\n  without repeating any change.",
    "96cb5b4e577ac03e6c2b706bb8de3eeb928832fda4c3dd1656681613fd90ace2": "##### Setup\n\n- **Blocked when:** never — running `fgos setup` always attempts its work;\n  a shell profile that does not exist is simply skipped rather than\n  refused.\n- **What changes:** for every shell profile the caller actually has (bash's\n  and/or zsh's, whichever exist), the shell helper's source line is added\n  if not already present — never duplicated on a repeat run. The local\n  project config file is also brought up to date: any setting present in\n  the current default schema but missing from the caller's file is added,\n  without ever changing a setting the caller already customized; a config\n  file that already has every current default is left untouched. The same\n  fill-missing-only treatment is applied to the **global** config file\n  (`~/.fgos/config.json`, tsk-1ri) — `fgos setup` initializes it with the\n  same default schema the project-local file gets, or fills in any missing\n  key, every time it runs, regardless of which project it's run from;\n  a value the caller already customized at the global level is never\n  overwritten. The lock directories of row 5g are created when missing:\n  `<lockRoot>` and `<fgosDir>/locks`, both mode 0700, an existing one tightened to it (the `lock-dirs` fix);\n  the `terminal` section of row 5h is one of the defaults the\n  fill-missing-only merge adds; `<stateRoot>/terminal` is not set up here: the\n  terminal allocation store creates it with mode 0700 on first use.\n- **Side effects:** the caller's own shell profile file(s), local project\n  config file, global config file (`~/.fgos/config.json`), and the two lock\n  directories may be created or modified; nothing outside the caller's own\n  environment is touched, and no network access happens.\n- **Afterwards:** the caller sees exactly what changed — which profile\n  file(s) gained the source line (or already had it), which project config\n  settings were newly added (or that no