    "9eec0db8f505988e14cd49afd54866aec765d4992349210f1bba248cefe1e933": "- **2026-10-23 — Third-party crates under a component contract are pinned exactly, with an upgrade record and acceptance tests (RUL107 (a third-party crate that a component's contract rests on is pinned exactly and has an upgrade record and acceptance tests)).** Before this, the YAML parser had a record and the validator crate was about to be the second case; the convention was in neither spec. Decision: the rule above. Evidence: `jsonschema` =0.60.0 reproduced three defects under the product's serde_json feature set that the default-feature evaluation did not show (an `unreachable!` on an out-of-range number, quadratic `pattern` compile time, wrong object comparison under `preserve_order`), each now guarded and covered by a test that fails when upstream fixes it (`packages/config/upgrade-record.md`, `packages/config/rust/tests/validator_acceptance.rs`). Rejected: an unwritten convention (the next crate's author would not know the bar).",
    "45ccc7989661f3fd7109d1e585b79ebbdf1223d00279819149037540bdc2ab2a": "- **2026-10-23 — Terminal setup loses a row and a fix.** The doctor row `terminal-config` is part of `terminal-control`, and the `terminal-dir` fix is gone: the allocation store creates `<stateRoot>/terminal` and everything under it with mode 0700 itself, so a repair that only recreated what the owner of the directory already creates was a second door. Source: `docs/platform/terminal-control/spec.md` §15 and the T2 arbitration `plans/reports/council-261023-0402-harden-t2-arbitration.md`.\n- **2026-10-23 — Every YAML parse goes through the native door (RUL108 (every YAML parse goes through the native door: one Rust parser behind src/util/yaml.mjs); supersedes RUL101 (fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; every YAML parse goes through one vendored js-yaml 5.4.3 behind src/util/yaml.mjs)).** Before this, a vendored, hash-pinned js-yaml 5.4.3 bundle was the Node parser because a bare checkout had no `npm install`, while the Rust side already read YAML through `fgos-yaml-doc`: two authorities for what YAML means. Decision: the one-door rule of RUL101 (every YAML parse goes through one vendored js-yaml) stays and the implementation behind the door becomes Rust, as the YAML Document spec (section 10) signed on 2026-10-22 says. `src/util/third-party/js-yaml.mjs` and its hash test are deleted in the same change; the upgrade-procedure paragraph of RUL101 (every YAML parse goes through one vendored js-yaml) no longer applies (the parser's own record is `packages/yaml-doc/upgrade-record.md`, RUL107 (a third-party crate under a component contract is pinned exactly with an upgrade record and acceptance tests)). Consequence, stated plainly: a process that cannot load the addon (a bare checkout without a staged addon, a channel that does not ship it) fails every YAML read with the door's named error; scripts that read YAML from a checkout stage the addon first (`ensureAddonForScript`), and a focused `node --test` needs the staged environment `npm test` builds. Evidence: the 136 golden cases of `packages/yaml-doc/contracts/fixtures/parse.v1.json` (every case of the old Node test lives there); a parity run of the old door against the new over 1414 tracked YAML and frontmatter documents found 1396 equal, 18 rejected by both, 0 different; `test/util/yaml.test.mjs` is the one Node wiring proof. Rejected: keeping js-yaml beside the crate (two authorities), a JSON projection plus a spawned process (owner 2026-10-22: 3 to 40 ms per call against 12 microseconds). Reversal condition: if the addon cannot be loaded on a channel the product must support, the fix is to ship the addon there, never a JavaScript parser.",
    "bd132225d6a3069a592a91c1b4e64cd72dab6964abe665baeec740779b69a0eb": "- **2026-10-21 — The npm channel's `fgos init` points to `fgctl init`.** Before this, a project onboarded with `npm install -g github:vantt/forgent` plus `fgos init`; that route needs Git through the native addon, which only an activated release ships (\"until M10\" in the old message). Git keeps one door, so the npm channel gets no addon-less Git read path: in a project no `fgctl init` has activated, `fgos init` refuses with `git-addon-unavailable: addon not shipped on this channel: onboard this project with fgctl init` and writes nothing. `test/setup/doctor-fresh-run.test.mjs` and `test/install-packaging.test.mjs` prove that refusal; the doctor state of an onboarded project is proven by the `fgctl init` tests (real runtime tail).",
    "0cba5ec1c8300186977eb36efbbd27374d081020df4ee4a7a6d154996679bb19": "- **2026-10-18 — Terminal-control enters setup and doctor.** `fgos setup` adds the `terminal` config section and creates `<stateRoot>/terminal` (0700); `fgos doctor` replaces `herdr-available` with `terminal-control`, `terminal-config` and `terminal-allocations`. Reasons: the herdr check now asks the port what the driver can do instead of running `herdr --version` on its own (one door); the new settings must be visible to doctor with their source or they are guesses (L5). Prior art read: the `herdr-available` check and `FGOS_HERDR_ANCHOR_PANE` diagnosis are kept inside `terminal-control`; `lock` is the precedent for a top-level section registered by a component. Decisions: (1) a missing herdr is a warning, not a failure, because direct mode does not use a terminal; (2) the doctor floor of 40x8 for `minCols`/`minRows` is the measured one from the measurement report; (3) the setting names reach the route and the failed-pane retention through `src/terminal-control/config.mjs`, so a value doctor accepts is the value a round uses; explicit values only are sent, the Rust core keeps its defaults.",
    "70f7f738b8518d874fbd06e5eaa083818ed8dcc2e6604e7fae20f5cf2aa3f1c0": "- **2026-10-16 — The activation, install and observe locks join holder-lock.** `fgctl`'s activation lock, the machine install lock and the observe store lock become kernel locks at `<fgosDir>/locks/activation.lock`, `<stateRoot>/locks/install.lock` and `<fgosDir>/locks/observe.lock`; the observe lock waits 5 s with no TTL and writes the shard snapshot under the lock. Their legacy paths (`.fgos/installation/activation.lock`, `<stateRoot>/install.lock`, `.fgos/observe/.lock`) become tombstone directories. Windows `fgctl init|upgrade|repair` refused with `lock-driver-unavailable` until the Windows driver landed (2026-10-17, `docs/platform/holder-lock/spec.md` §8.1). (The `lock-fences` check and fix that covered these rows were removed on 2026-10-22.) Details: RUL18 (startup reap only takes claims the runner itself made, never a human or session holder) above and `docs/platform/holder-lock/spec.md` §15.",
    "67e08a40e9c121640c429e5929a6680f399e1bd157ca13375a705e6750095596": "- **2026-10-16 — Holder-lock enters setup, doctor and the activation gate (RUL18 (`fgctl` activates a release only after the holder-lock semantics probe and the borrow vectors are green)).** The cold locks of the runner (gateway, runner, sessions, advance, reconcile, recovery, full-suite, provider account and state, dispatch cwd) move from pid/TTL files to kernel locks on open file descriptions (`docs/platform/holder-lock/spec.md`). Owner decisions of 2026-10-16: a stop-everything cutover with fences instead of a two-protocol transition, so no marker and no protocol floor in the manifest; Windows reports `unsupported` for these locks rather than falling back to the old files; macOS runs the `flock` driver at once, gated at activation by the vectors and the probe. In this spec: six doctor checks (`lock-kernel-semantics`, `lock-borrow-vectors`, `lock-holders`, `lock-fences`, `lock-predecessor-kill-wait`, `state-root`; three remain since 2026-10-22) and the `lock-fences` fix replace `provider-capacity-lock-stale`, whose ledger is no longer a mutex; `fgos setup` creates `<lockRoot>` and `<fgosDir>/locks` and registered the config key `lock.predecessorKillWaitMs` (removed on 2026-10-22; the wait is fixed at 2000 ms). Plan: `plans/261016-1104-holder-lock-kernel-lock-foundation/plan.md`.",
    "4599fc3ec109b2d879d8b76e5a841b4452d69865da0f4517e5a267003628c104": "- **2026-10-12 — `fgctl init` downloads by default (RUL15 (fgctl init downloads a checksum-verified release when no source is given)).** Before this, a plain `fgctl init` in a project with no pin failed with \"`--from <source>` is required\", so a first install meant downloading the release tarball by hand. Owner decision: no source and no pin means the latest GitHub release; `FGCTL_VERSION` selects an exact tag; `FGCTL_FROM` and `--from` select a non-release build; checksum verification is mandatory; the transport is the system `curl` rather than a new HTTP crate (same behavior and override as `install.sh`, no TLS stack to carry in `fgctl`). Prior art: `fgctl init --from` (the release-asset door, kept unchanged and now equivalent to `FGCTL_FROM`), `install.sh`'s `FGCTL_VERSION`/`FGCTL_ASSET_BASE_URL`/`FGCTL_TARGET` (reused, not redefined). Same day, follow-up: the pin now records the tag, so a pinned workspace whose release is not staged on this machine downloads it instead of needing a source; only a digest-only legacy pin still needs `FGCTL_FROM`/`FGCTL_VERSION`/`--from`, because a digest names no download. Follow-up (2026-10-14): `fgctl upgrade` uses the same verified download pipeline (`--from` > `FGCTL_FROM` > `FGCTL_VERSION` > latest GitHub release) and records the downloaded tag in `.fgos/distribution.json`, so an upgraded project's pin keeps a downloadable `releaseVersion` for teammates instead of reverting to digest-only.\n- **2026-10-13 — Windows is a native release target (RUL16 (Windows is a native release target: same assets, an installer and launchers that need no POSIX shell)).** Owner decision: the level is \"native, complete\" (PowerShell and `cmd.exe`, no Git Bash, no WSL), with `tar.gz` kept as the single archive format and macOS left out of scope. Prior art, reused: the Windows CI hardening track made the Rust workspace build and the release-tree staging work on Windows (the `fgos.exe` entry, colon-free release store directory names, drive-letter-safe archive validation), and an earlier change wrote `.cmd` launchers on Windows that ran the `sh` shims through a `sh` on `PATH`; that required Git for Windows, so it is replaced by launchers that read `activation.json` themselves. Missing before: a Windows release asset, `install.ps1`, a Windows consumer proof, and any check that the launchers exist. Also in this change: the Linux asset moves from `ubuntu-latest` (binaries then needed glibc 2.39 and printed a loader warning on Ubuntu 22.04, glibc 2.35) to `ubuntu-22.04`, with a CI gate on the glibc symbol versions.\n- **2026-10-16 — fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; YAML is read through one vendored parser (RUL101 (fgOS has no npm runtime dependency; every YAML parse goes through one vendored js-yaml 5.4.3 behind src/util/yaml.mjs)).** Before this, `yaml` was the one production dependency. An installed release carried it (the release builder stages declared production dependencies), but a checkout, linked worktree or unpacked copy without `npm install` did not, so every module importing it failed to load there, and the doctor and setup paths grew their own YAML readers and \"not evaluated\" branches to keep loading. Decision: `package.json#dependencies` stays empty; the only YAML parser is `src/util/third-party/js-yaml.mjs`, the ESM bundle from the js-yaml 5.4.3 tarball, unmodified except a header and the dropped source-map comment line, and every parse goes through `src/util/yaml.mjs` (`parseYaml(text, { filename })`: zero documents give `null`, more than one throws, `maxAliases` 100, js-yaml's default core schema). An architecture test fails on any other import of `yaml`, `js-yaml` or the third-party file. Rejected: keeping `yaml` and making doctor tolerate its absence (what the workarounds did, and the cause of the divergence); a hand-rolled parser (a second authority for what YAML means); `vendor/` or `dist/` as the location (the repository's scout-block hook refuses those path segments, so agents could not read or review the file); 5.4.2 (a block-indentation mis-parse fixed in 5.4.3) and 5.5.0 (one day old at the time). Readers that used to skip silently now report a parse failure as a named problem with file, line and column; pinned persona text that the strict parser rejects renders through a private legacy projection so older runs resume with the same text (its removal is tracked in the backlog). Evidence: commits `8ef1baf1c` (door and vendored file), `838b044e5` (call sites), `b75c01a3f` (release and dev-host staging), `6013ca389` (dependency dropped), `f96eece77`, `b7eeb4bb6` and `26b3338a7` (readers use the parser), `f705b9c8c` (doctor checks `compiled.json`); a parity run of the old parser against the door over every tracked YAML and frontmatter document found 828 documents, 0 differences, 13 rejected by both; a cold `fgos` start costs 2 to 3 ms more (97 to 99 ms for `version`, 109 to 110 ms for `dispatch decide`); the full suite ran 7850 tests with 0 failures with release binaries present; `test/util/yaml.test.mjs` pins the vendored bytes by hash. Kept on their own paths: `src/report/frontmatter.mjs` pairs `parseFrontmatter` with `renderFrontmatter` over a deliberately flat schema, so replacing only the parser would make the pair asymmetric; `distill.mjs` carries a minimal YAML reader because portable skills ship without `src/`; `domains/*/compiled.json` stays a generated projection because parsing domain YAML at import would put every verb, hook and `doctor` at the mercy of one YAML typo (doctor now checks its freshness instead). Upgrade procedure: in an empty scratch directory run `npm pack js-yaml@<version> --ignore-scripts`; compare the tarball's sha512 with `npm view js-yaml@<version> dist.integrity`; extract the `.mjs` bundle from the tarball without executing anything in it (build the path so the hook-refused segment does not appear in the command); confirm it has no import or require; diff it against the current bundle and read every changed line; re-run the parity comparison against the previous parser over the tracked YAML; replace the third-party file with the new bundle under a fresh header (source, version, integrity, license, hash); update the hash constant in `test/util/yaml.test.mjs`. js-yaml advisories are watched through the backlog, since `npm audit` no longer covers the file.\n- **2026-10-17 — Instruction composition belongs to the Agent Context Engine; packaging-distribution delivers (RUL102 (one owner for composing agent instructions: the Agent Context Engine composes and selects rules, packaging-distribution only discovers, installs, renders per host and repairs)).** Before this, two documents gave the same authority to two components: `docs/platform/packaging-distribution/contracts/instruction-composition-and-projection.md` §2 named packaging-distribution owner of the composition engine and registry shape, while `docs/platform/proposals/documentation-system-unification.md` §4.7.2-4.7.3 put the Instruction Composer in the Agent Context Engine and said packaging-distribution \"does not decide semantic composition\". Left as is, a future Agent Context Engine would be built beside `src/setup/instruction-composition.mjs`, giving two composers and two orderings of the same rules. Owner decision: composing and selecting rules (kind order, override, conflict, per-task selection, per-host budget, progressive disclosure) has one owner, the Agent Context Engine; packaging-distribution keeps delivery (source discovery and install, per-host render adapters, projection ledger, doctor repair). Two delivery moments follow: static delivery, where packaging-distribution asks the engine for each host's effective set when sources change or at setup/install/upgrade and renders it to files; and per-task delivery, where the runner asks the engine for a context packet that goes into the worker brief, with no file written. The engine knows no host or file. Rejected: packaging-distribution keeping composition and also absorbing budgets and per-task selection (it would grow a content-deciding role inside an install/delivery component). Weight guard: no new component now; the existing code in `src/setup/instruction-registry.mjs` and `src/setup/instruction-composition.mjs` is the engine's seed and moves to its own module only when the first budget/tiering slice lands, in the same change, never kept in two places. Evidence and reasoning: `plans/reports/research-261016-1118-agent-code-quality-guardrails.md` sections 2d and 6.",
    "17e86891b31441849e0a2d91751b94f2c01fb3fe13a987609e5f58a30816e212": "- **2026-10-12 — `fgctl init` downloads by default (RUL15 (fgctl init downloads a checksum-verified release when no source is given)).** Before this, a plain `fgctl init` in a project with no pin failed with \"`--from <source>` is required\", so a first install meant downloading the release tarball by hand. Owner decision: no source and no pin means the latest GitHub release; `FGCTL_VERSION` selects an exact tag; `FGCTL_FROM` and `--from` select a non-release build; checksum verification is mandatory; the transport is the system `curl` rather than a new HTTP crate (same behavior and override as `install.sh`, no TLS stack to carry in `fgctl`). Prior art: `fgctl init --from` (the release-asset door, kept unchanged and now equivalent to `FGCTL_FROM`), `install.sh`'s `FGCTL_VERSION`/`FGCTL_ASSET_BASE_URL`/`FGCTL_TARGET` (reused, not redefined). Same day, follow-up: the pin now records the tag, so a pinned workspace whose release is not staged on this machine downloads it instead of needing a source; only a digest-only legacy pin still needs `FGCTL_FROM`/`FGCTL_VERSION`/`--from`, because a digest names no download. Follow-up (2026-10-14): `fgctl upgrade` uses the same verified download pipeline (`--from` > `FGCTL_FROM` > `FGCTL_VERSION` > latest GitHub release) and records the downloaded tag in `.fgos/distribution.json`, so an upgraded project's pin keeps a downloadable `releaseVersion` for teammates instead of reverting to digest-only.\n- **2026-10-13 — Windows is a native release target (RUL16 (Windows is a native release target: same assets, an installer and launchers that need no POSIX shell)).** Owner decision: the level is \"native, complete\" (PowerShell and `cmd.exe`, no Git Bash, no WSL), with `tar.gz` kept as the single archive format and macOS left out of scope. Prior art, reused: the Windows CI hardening track made the Rust workspace build and the release-tree staging work on Windows (th